    <repair-steps>
        <post-migration>
            <!--
                decidesk -> decidiq app-id rename (Phase 3). Both steps MUST stay
                ahead of InitializeSettings.

                There is no in-place app-id upgrade in Nextcloud: `oc_appconfig`
                and `oc_preferences` are namespaced by app id, so renaming <id>
                does not rename the rows — it makes this app ask for its data
                under a name nothing answers to.

                The ordering is load-bearing rather than tidy. InitializeSettings
                GENERATES a fresh `voter_token_secret` when it finds none, and
                that value is the HMAC key signing every voting token and
                mail-reply link. If it ran first it would mint a new key over the
                orphaned old one and silently invalidate every outstanding vote
                link — so the copy has to happen before it looks.

                Both steps are idempotent and non-destructive: they never
                overwrite a value already present under the new id and never
                delete the old rows, so a rollback still finds its data.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
            <!--
                Third leg of the rename, on the OpenRegister register ROW. The
                slug moves too (`decidesk` -> `decidiq`), together with
                `x-openregister.app`, which for a `type: application`
                configuration IS a register slug rather than an attribution
                label — ImportHandler::autoCreateRegisterIfApplication() reads
                `$xOpenregister['app'] ?? $appId` as the slug.

                MUST run before InitializeSettings, which triggers the import.
                OpenRegister resolves a register by slug and its not-found branch
                CREATES a second, empty register rather than failing, so an
                import that ran first would fork the register and leave every
                stored object behind on the old row, silently.

                It moves no data: an object is bound to its register by NUMERIC
                id (`_register` in every shard table, and the tables are named
                `oc_openregister_table_<registerId>_<schemaId>`), so this is one
                column on one row. Idempotent, non-destructive, and it refuses
                rather than merges if both slugs already exist.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
            <!--
                SIBLING OF MigrateRegisterSlug, AND NEITHER COVERS THE OTHER.
                OpenRegister resolves the two halves of a configuration by
                DIFFERENT keys: a REGISTER by slug alone (RegisterMapper::find),
                a SCHEMA by the PAIR —
                SchemaMapper::findByApplicationAndSlug(slug, application).

                This app now passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `decidiq`,
                while every schema it already owns still carries
                `application = decidesk`, so the pair matches nothing — and
                ImportHandler's not-found branch is not an error path, it is the
                "create a new one" path. The import InitializeSettings triggers
                would build a SECOND, EMPTY schema set under the new application
                id while every stored object stayed bound to the old rows.
                Nothing errors; the app renders empty collections.

                MUST run before InitializeSettings, for the same reason
                MigrateRegisterSlug does — the import is the thing that forks.

                Measured on a live install: 98 schemas still under `decidesk`,
                and zero of their slugs collide with a twin under `decidiq`, so
                all 98 move.

                Where a slug DOES already have a twin under the new application
                id it refuses rather than merges: two rows sharing
                (application, slug) means findByApplicationAndSlug(), which caps
                at one row, silently picks one and the other's objects become
                unreachable. That is a decision about data, not a migration.

                Never deletes a schema, and never throws — it runs under
                <install>, where an escaping exception aborts the install and
                the app never enables.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\InitializeSettings</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register. The schema edit
                changes the DECLARATION; every row already written still holds
                the Dutch string, and a filter on the new value returns null
                rather than an error. The Dutch legal terms themselves are not
                lost — they live in l10n/nl.json against their English keys.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\RenameDutchDecidiqValues</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateEmailLinksToRegistry</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateCommentsToTalkLeaf</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateActionItemsToDeckLeaf</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\ProjectGovernanceRoleScopes</step>
            <!--
                Must run AFTER the register sync that adds the English columns, so it
                can tell the rename case from the back-fill case. Scoped to the
                `decidiq` register only — several of these words (onderwerp) also
                exist in dossiq's. The register SLUG moves with the app id, and
                MigrateRegisterSlug above renames the row before anything here
                resolves it; renaming the slug WITHOUT that step is what would
                import a fresh empty register and orphan every stored object.
                Idempotent and non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\RenameDutchVocabularyColumns</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (68-unified-decision-templates.json's
                decision-template schema + built-in seeds must already exist). Repairs live
                process-template/vve-decision-template objects into decision-template objects
                (unified-decision-templates, ADR-032 chain link 1 of 3). Idempotent (matched by
                migratedFrom.sourceUuid) and non-destructive — never deletes or edits a source object.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateLegacyTemplatesToDecisionTemplate</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (74-generic-body-configuration.json's
                body-governance-configuration schema must already exist). Copies vve-configuration
                rows onto the generic schema (generic-body-configuration). Idempotent by GOVERNANCE
                BODY rather than by source uuid: the schema declares exactly one configuration per
                body, so the body IS the identity, and keying on the source row would let a re-seed
                create a second configuration for the same body — the defect that duplicated every
                built-in decision template. Non-destructive: never deletes or edits a source row.
                <post-migration> only, like the step above: a fresh install has no legacy rows, and
                the example sets seed the GENERIC schema directly.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateVveToBodyConfiguration</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (75-generic-audit-statement.json's
                audit-statement schema must already exist). Copies kascommissie-verklaring rows
                onto the generic schema (generic-audit-statement). Identity is (governance body,
                financial year) rather than the source uuid: a body files one statement per year,
                so that pair is what makes two rows the same record. Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateKascommissieToAuditStatement</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (76-questions-as-agenda-items.json
                must already have retired the three source schemas). Copies mondelinge-vraag and
                interpellatieverzoek rows onto the generic agenda-item, carrying each row's
                distinctive fields in `typeFields` and its kind in a per-body agenda-item-type,
                then folds vragenuur-configuratie onto those types. Identity is the SOURCE object's
                uuid, recorded as `typeFields.migratedFromObject`: unlike a body configuration a
                body has many questions, so the body cannot be the identity and only the source row
                can. Non-destructive: never deletes or edits a source row.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateQuestionsToAgendaItems</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (77-fold-regulations-into-governing-
                documents.json must already have added the absorbed properties to GoverningDocument,
                or every copied row fails validation on them). Copies regeling and regeling-versie
                rows onto governing-document and governing-document-versie. Documents FIRST, then
                versions: a version references its document, so the parents must exist and be
                indexable before the children can point at them. Identity is the SOURCE object's
                uuid, recorded in the declared `migratedFromObject` property. Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateRegulationsToGoverningDocuments</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (79-one-consultation-schema.json must
                already have declared `consultation` and `consultation-response`, AND the register's
                own `schemas` list must carry both slugs, or every write throws "schema not carried
                by register" and this step downgrades it to a warning nobody reads). Copies three
                pairs onto one: advice-request/advies, zienswijzeronde/zienswijze and
                member-consultation/member-consultation-response. Asks FIRST, then answers: an
                answer references its ask. Identity is the SOURCE object's uuid, in the declared
                `migratedFromObject` property. Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateConsultationsToOneSchema</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (80-retire-the-unbuilt-rooster.json
                must already have added `notes` and `migratedFromObject` to PositionType, or every
                copied row fails validation on them). Copies termijn-regeling rows onto position-type:
                a term rule is a property of a position, and PositionType already declared
                termDurationMonths, maxConsecutiveTerms and reappointable. The role ENUM becomes the
                position's NAME. Identity is the SOURCE object's uuid. Non-destructive.

                rooster-van-aftreden and rooster-regel are deliberately NOT migrated: both were a
                projection whose generator (RoosterService) was never written, and PositionHold
                requires a startDate a rooster regel never recorded, so any value written would be
                invented. Their rows stay readable under their original schemas.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateTermRulesToPositionTypes</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (81-integrity-disclosures-in-plain-words
                .json must already declare ancillary-position and declared-gift, AND the register's
                own `schemas` list must carry both slugs, or every write throws "schema not carried by
                register" and this step downgrades it to a warning nobody reads). Copies nevenfunctie
                and geschenk rows across unchanged except for the schema, resolving every reference to
                a uuid first; every property keeps its name because the properties were already in
                plain words. Then folds integriteitsbeleid onto body-governance-configuration, which
                is keyed on the SAME body: an existing configuration is UPDATED rather than replaced,
                so the constitutive document and majority overrides another change wrote survive.
                Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateIntegrityDisclosures</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (82-planning-cycle-in-plain-words.json
                must already declare the three planning-cycle schemas, AND the register's own
                `schemas` list must carry all three slugs). Copies pc-cyclus, cyclus-template and
                cyclus-stap across in DEPENDENCY ORDER: templates, then cycles, then steps, because a
                cycle references its template and a step references its cycle. A reference to a copied
                row FOLLOWS it to the new record; without that a step would live on the new schema
                while pointing at the retired cycle, which is readable, plausible, and joined to the
                wrong side of the migration. `cyclus` becomes `cycle`, the one property that was
                itself Dutch. Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigratePlanningCycles</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (83-confidentiality-in-plain-words.json
                must already declare both schemas, AND the register's own `schemas` list must carry
                both slugs). Copies geheimhouding-grond and geheimhouding across in that ORDER: a
                restriction names the ground it was imposed on, so the grounds must exist and be
                indexable first, and the `ground` reference is retargeted at the COPY. Without that a
                restriction would live on the new schema while pointing at the retired ground.
                Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateConfidentialityRecords</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (84-commitment-in-plain-words.json must
                already declare Commitment, AND the register's own `schemas` list must carry
                governance-commitment). Copies toezegging rows across unchanged except for the schema,
                resolving directedTo/madeBy/meeting/agendaItem to uuids first because a SEEDED row
                holds slugs and saveObject() rejects those outright. Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateCommitments</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (85-documents-as-agenda-items.json must
                already have retired the three source schemas). Copies ingekomen-stuk,
                raadsinformatiebrief and technische-vraag onto agenda-item, each carrying its
                distinctive fields in `typeFields` and its kind in a per-body agenda-item-type. LETTERS
                BEFORE QUESTIONS: a technical question's parent is a letter, so the letters must exist
                and be indexable before the questions that hang off them, and the question's `rib` is
                retargeted at the letter's COPY. Identity is the SOURCE object's uuid in
                `typeFields.migratedFromObject`. Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateDocumentsToAgendaItems</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register (86-the-last-two-dutch-names.json must
                already declare both schemas, AND the register's own `schemas` list must carry both
                slugs). Copies termijnagenda-item and bevoegdheidstoedeling rows across, renaming the
                FOUR properties that were themselves Dutch: delegans, delegansDescription,
                delegatarisBody and beperkingen. A value written under an old key would land on a
                property the target does not declare, which OpenRegister stores nowhere while
                answering 200. Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateTheLastTwoDutchNames</step>
            <!--
                member-onboarding-in-plain-words: onboarding-traject and offboarding-traject were
                the last Dutch-named schemas in the app, and the-last-two-dutch-names said there
                were none left. Copies their rows onto member-onboarding and member-offboarding,
                renaming three properties with them: the Dutch-spelled beedigingsType becomes
                installationType, swearingInDate becomes installedOn, and swearingInMeeting becomes
                installationMeeting. That last one is a reference AND a rename, so its value is
                resolved as a reference and written under the new key. A value written under an old
                key lands on a property the target does not declare, which OpenRegister stores
                nowhere while answering 200. Non-destructive.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\MigrateMemberOnboarding</step>
            <!--
                plain-words-for-groups-and-the-installation-step: swearing-in is a council's
                ceremony; a board signs a deed instead. The stepType enum now says installation, so
                stored steps have to follow or they hold a value the schema no longer offers. NOT in
                RenameDutchDecidiqValues, which exists for exactly this: that step rewrites by
                DATABASE COLUMN, and stepType is not a column, it sits inside the steps array of an
                object payload. Adding it there would have planned nothing and reported success.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\RenameStepTypes</step>
            <!--
                model-debt-cleanup-code: both steps below run AFTER InitializeSettings so they
                read the NEW $ref declarations (Participant -> Membership/Person retargets,
                model-debt-cleanup-schema fragment 67) to know what a valid target type looks
                like. RepointConflictOfInterestBoardMember repoints ConflictOfInterest.boardMember
                from Participant to Membership; MigrateBoardProxyToProxyAuthorization runs after
                it, migrating retired board-proxy rows into proxyAuthorization objects via the
                same crosswalk resolver. Both idempotent and non-destructive — see migration.md.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\RepointConflictOfInterestBoardMember</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateBoardProxyToProxyAuthorization</step>
            <!--
                decision-types-as-configuration: writes the shipped decisionType
                vocabulary into the `decision_types` app-config row, once. After
                that the row is the ONLY authority; the schema declarations carry
                no enum and DecisionTypeRegistry validates against the row. Never
                overwrites a stored vocabulary, so an admin's additions and
                removals survive every upgrade. Ordering is free: it touches app
                config only, and until it runs DecisionTypeRegistry falls back to
                the same shipped list, so nothing fails closed in the gap.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\SeedDecisionTypes</step>
            <!--
                parafering-route-runtime label repair: instantiate() used to
                write '' for route steps without labels, OpenRegister stored
                NULL, and the required `label` then 400'd the patch recording
                every advance — cross-app routes wedged on their first sign-off.
                Backfills a derived label (stage type + step number) onto every
                stage stored without one, via the engine so the derivation is
                the same one instantiate() now uses. Idempotent, and it KEEPS
                the orphan approval-action rows the defect appended: they are
                the audit record of what the signer did. <post-migration> only —
                a fresh install has no stages to repair.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Migration\RepairDecisionStageLabels</step>
            <!--
                Fifth leg of the rename, on the ADMINISTRATORS GROUP. The
                register's authorization baseline named `decidesk-administrators`
                and OpenRegister's GroupProvisioner creates every group an
                authorization block names, so existing installs hold the group —
                and its hand-granted memberships — under the OLD app id. The
                baseline now names `decidiq-administrators` first (the old id
                stays honored); this step copies the old group's members into
                the new one. After InitializeSettings on purpose: the import it
                triggers provisions the new group, so this step normally only
                copies members. No rename exists for a Nextcloud gid, and the
                old group is never deleted and never loses a member — same
                rollback posture as the other rename legs. Idempotent.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateAdminGroup</step>
            <!--
                plain-words-for-groups-and-the-installation-step: griffie is a Dutch council's clerk
                office and decidesk-integriteit is that word in Dutch under the old app id. Both were
                recipient groups on schema notifications. Memberships are COPIED onto
                decidiq-secretariat and decidiq-integrity; the old groups are never deleted or
                emptied, so an admin with automation pointing at one keeps a working group.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateGovernanceGroupNames</step>
        </post-migration>
        <install>
            <!--
                <install> is the path a REAL deployment takes, because Nextcloud
                discovers `decidiq` as a new app rather than as an upgrade of
                `decidesk` — so registering the two rename steps under
                <post-migration> alone would mean they never run where it
                matters. Same ordering rule as above: ahead of
                InitializeSettings, for the voter_token_secret reason.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
            <!--
                Third leg of the rename, on the OpenRegister register ROW. The
                slug moves too (`decidesk` -> `decidiq`), together with
                `x-openregister.app`, which for a `type: application`
                configuration IS a register slug rather than an attribution
                label — ImportHandler::autoCreateRegisterIfApplication() reads
                `$xOpenregister['app'] ?? $appId` as the slug.

                MUST run before InitializeSettings, which triggers the import.
                OpenRegister resolves a register by slug and its not-found branch
                CREATES a second, empty register rather than failing, so an
                import that ran first would fork the register and leave every
                stored object behind on the old row, silently.

                It moves no data: an object is bound to its register by NUMERIC
                id (`_register` in every shard table, and the tables are named
                `oc_openregister_table_<registerId>_<schemaId>`), so this is one
                column on one row. Idempotent, non-destructive, and it refuses
                rather than merges if both slugs already exist.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
            <!--
                SIBLING OF MigrateRegisterSlug, AND NEITHER COVERS THE OTHER.
                OpenRegister resolves the two halves of a configuration by
                DIFFERENT keys: a REGISTER by slug alone (RegisterMapper::find),
                a SCHEMA by the PAIR —
                SchemaMapper::findByApplicationAndSlug(slug, application).

                This app now passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `decidiq`,
                while every schema it already owns still carries
                `application = decidesk`, so the pair matches nothing — and
                ImportHandler's not-found branch is not an error path, it is the
                "create a new one" path. The import InitializeSettings triggers
                would build a SECOND, EMPTY schema set under the new application
                id while every stored object stayed bound to the old rows.
                Nothing errors; the app renders empty collections.

                MUST run before InitializeSettings, for the same reason
                MigrateRegisterSlug does — the import is the thing that forks.

                Measured on a live install: 98 schemas still under `decidesk`,
                and zero of their slugs collide with a twin under `decidiq`, so
                all 98 move.

                Where a slug DOES already have a twin under the new application
                id it refuses rather than merges: two rows sharing
                (application, slug) means findByApplicationAndSlug(), which caps
                at one row, silently picks one and the other's objects become
                unreachable. That is a decision about data, not a migration.

                Never deletes a schema, and never throws — it runs under
                <install>, where an escaping exception aborts the install and
                the app never enables.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\InitializeSettings</step>
            <!--
                After InitializeSettings imports the register. The schema edit
                changes the DECLARATION; every row already written still holds
                the Dutch string, and a filter on the new value returns null
                rather than an error. The Dutch legal terms themselves are not
                lost — they live in l10n/nl.json against their English keys.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\RenameDutchDecidiqValues</step>
            <!--
                decision-types-as-configuration: same step as in
                <post-migration>, for the same reason the rename steps appear in
                both blocks — a fresh install never runs <post-migration>, and a
                fresh install is exactly where the vocabulary row does not exist
                yet. Idempotent; never overwrites a stored vocabulary.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\SeedDecisionTypes</step>
            <!--
                Same step as in <post-migration>, for the same reason the other
                rename legs appear in both blocks: <install> is the path a real
                deployment takes, because Nextcloud discovers `decidiq` as a NEW
                app rather than as an upgrade of `decidesk` — and an instance
                that ran the old app is exactly where `decidesk-administrators`
                exists with members to carry over. On a genuinely fresh install
                the old group does not exist and the step is a no-op; the new
                group is provisioned (empty) by the register import instead.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateAdminGroup</step>
            <!--
                plain-words-for-groups-and-the-installation-step: griffie is a Dutch council's clerk
                office and decidesk-integriteit is that word in Dutch under the old app id. Both were
                recipient groups on schema notifications. Memberships are COPIED onto
                decidiq-secretariat and decidiq-integrity; the old groups are never deleted or
                emptied, so an admin with automation pointing at one keeps a working group.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Decidiq\Repair\MigrateGovernanceGroupNames</step>
        </install>
    </repair-steps>

